{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068481 :question/math-academy-id "e-14010" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1$ sketched below.\n\n![](images/e6/e63b2ac5fe2bdfb905fe3c86706be4a556e5322698f9fd26ee115dba0a886f4b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=5x+3 \\\\ y &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+3$ into the second equation $y=x-1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =x-1 \\\\ 5x & =x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1, \\, y=-2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-2).$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069112 :question/math-academy-id "q-208830" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3x-1 \\\\ y &=-4x+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=3x-1$ into the second equation $y=-4x+6$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =-4x+6 \\\\ 3x & =-4x+7 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,1,2\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069113 :question/math-academy-id "q-208840" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y = 3x - 2$ and $y =-5x + 2$ has coordinates ({{field-1}}, {{field-2}})."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069258 :question/math-academy-id "q-3319" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y =-10x - 3$ and $y =-8x - 7$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531570 :question/math-academy-id "q-12345" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=4x+3 \\\\ y &=-x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=4x+3$ into the second equation $y=-x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & =-x-2 \\\\ 5x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=-x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x-2 \\\\  & =-(-1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1$ and $y=-1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531619 :question/math-academy-id "q-215406" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=5x+2 \\\\ y &=-2x-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+2$ into the second equation $y=-2x-5$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & =-2x-5 \\\\ 5x & =-2x-7 \\\\ 7x & =-7 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=5x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x+2 \\\\  & =5(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-5+2 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1,\\,y=-3.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,-1,-3\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531659 :question/math-academy-id "q-12341" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-3x+4 \\\\ y &=3x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=-3x+4$ into the second equation $y=3x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =3x-2 \\\\ -6x & =-6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(1)-2 \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are  $(1,1).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068482 :question/math-academy-id "e-14012" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2 y = 1$ and $2 x - y = 7.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ 2x-y &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ term in the first equation:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 && \\\\ 2x-y &=7 && |\\:\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ 4x-2y &=14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{2y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ (x+4x) & =(1+14) \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=3$ into the equation $x+2y=1$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =1 \\\\ 3+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-2 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=3, \\, y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(3,-1).$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069114 :question/math-academy-id "q-208847" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $5x - 2y = 3$ and $3x - y = 3$ has coordinates ({{field-1}}, {{field-2}})."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069128 :question/math-academy-id "q-215423" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=7$ and $3x+2y=11$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=7 \\\\ 3x+2y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=7 && |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ 3x+2y &=11 && \\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=14 \\\\ 3x+2y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ (2x+3x) & =(14+11) \\\\ 5x & =25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=5$ into the equation $x-y=7$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =7 \\\\ 5-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=5,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(5,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069518 :question/math-academy-id "q-75876" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x + y = 14$ and $2x - y = 6$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531558 :question/math-academy-id "q-215907" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=6$  and $2x+3y=2$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=6 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $3,$ then we will get a $-3y$ term that can cancel with the $3y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=6 && |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y &=18 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ 2x+\\cancel{3y} & =2 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(18+2) \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=4$ back into the equation $x-y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =6 \\\\ 4-y & =6 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=4,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(4,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531585 :question/math-academy-id "q-215425" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $4x+3y=5$ and $-x-6y=4$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y &=5 \\\\ -x-6y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $6y$ term that can cancel with the $-6y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y &=5 && |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ -x-6y &=4 && \\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x+6y &=10 \\\\ -x-6y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+\\cancel{6y} & =10 \\\\ -x-\\cancel{6y} & =4 \\\\ (8x-x) & =(10+4) \\\\ 7x & =14 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $4x+3y=5$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y & =5 \\\\ 4(2)+3y & =5 \\\\ 8+3y & =5 \\\\ 3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=2,\\,y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(2,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561036 :question/math-academy-id "q-60172" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x+4y=1$ and $2x-2y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=1 \\\\ 2x-2y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-4y$ term that can cancel with the $4y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=1 &&  \\\\ 2x-2y &=3 && |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=1 \\\\ 4x-4y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ (3x+4x) & =(1+6) \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=1$ into the equation $2x-2y=3$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-2y & =3 \\\\ 2-2y & =3 \\\\ -2y & =1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561054 :question/math-academy-id "q-75873" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $2x+y=12$ and $x-2y=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y &=12 \\\\ x-2y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $2y$ term that can cancel with the $-2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y &=12 && |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ x-2y &=6 && \\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=24 \\\\ x-2y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\cancel{2y} & =24 \\\\ x-\\cancel{2y} & =6 \\\\ (4x+x) & =(24+6) \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=6$ into the equation $x-2y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =6 \\\\ 6-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=6,\\,y=0.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(6,0).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068483 :question/math-academy-id "e-14015" :question/problem "The lines $y = -3 x + a$ and $y = x - 1$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b, -4).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(b,-4)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b, \\, y=-4$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-3x+a \\\\ y &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b, \\, y=-4$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4 &=-3b+a \\\\ -4 &=b-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn the second equation, we can solve for $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =b-1 \\\\ -3 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $a\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-3b+a \\\\ -4 & =-3(-3)+a \\\\ -4 & =9+a \\\\ -13 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -13$ and $b = -3.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069129 :question/math-academy-id "q-215453" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $3x + y = 8$ and $-2x + y = a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(1, b)$.\n\n$a =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, b =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069180 :question/math-academy-id "q-24481" :question/problem "The lines $y = x + 1$ and $y = 3x - a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(1, b)$. Find the parameters $a$ and $b$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531564 :question/math-academy-id "q-215456" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $4x+y=a$ and $7x+2y=-2$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(-2,b).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(-2,b)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=-2,\\,y=b$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y &=a \\\\ 7x+2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=-2,\\,y=b$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+b &=a \\\\ 7(-2)+2b &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8+b &=a \\\\ b &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we know that $b=6.$ Then, we can substitute $b=6$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+b & =a \\\\ -8+6 & =a \\\\ -2 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531607 :question/math-academy-id "q-215449" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $y=-2x+2$ and $y=-5x-a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b,8).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(b,8)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b,\\,y=8$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2x+2 \\\\ y &=-5x-a\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b,\\,y=8$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8 &=-2(b)+2 \\\\ 8 &=-5(b)-a\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8 &=-2b+2 \\\\ 8 &=-5b-a\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $b=-3.$ Then, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =-5b-a \\\\ 8 & =-5(-3)-a \\\\ 8 & =15-a \\\\ -7 & =-a \\\\ 7 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7$ and $b=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561058 :question/math-academy-id "q-60182" :question/problem "The lines $y=3x-b$ and $y=x+5$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(a,2).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(a,2)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=a,\\,y=2$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3x-b \\\\ y &=x+5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=a,\\,y=2$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=3a-b \\\\ 2 &=a+5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =a+5 \\\\ -3 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3a-b \\\\ 2 & =3(-3)-b \\\\ 2 & =-9-b \\\\ 11 & =-b \\\\ -11 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $b=-11.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Parameters Given a Point of Intersection"}] :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"}
